But the Dev Team could also be considered to have shown resistance to
the full embrace of open source software, and that could also be the
cause of some of those difficulties.

Since the heady days of Nethack's origins, Linux has come into being
and matured, and it has demonstrated the benefits of having a
completely open (indeed, even loud-mouthed) development team, and
public, vocal discussion on development issues.  There are certainly
drawbacks to such an approach (as Slashdot's main page illustrates),
and when the project in question is a computer game much of whose
appeal comes from a sense of mystery, it is understood why the Dev Team
may want to be silent.  Silence is not a very open sourcey policy,
however.

Some of the work of combining patches could possibly be done by the
patch authors themselves, if they had more information concerning what
the team wants to do with the game.  It's difficult for a patch author
to ensure compatibility with every other patch, upgrade that to
impossible as the number of patches scales up, and it's wasted work to
do all that if a patch has no chance of being taken up into the main
code.  If those authors knew what the Dev Team thought of various
patches and their liklihood of being incorporated into the game, they
could do some of that work themselves.  They could even take upcoming
features into account

The result: some of the difficulty the Dev Team might have in combining
new features from user patches to the game is a result of their
historical, mysterious silence.

